An example of offline privacy is shredders.
There are several types of shredders commercially available.

The first kind is strip cut. Strip cut shredders (DIN P-1 and P-2) have a single blade that cut the paper into long, vertical strips. It should be noted however,
that it is easy, with some effort, to put documents back together with these strips. This means that strip cut shredders are still vulnerable, and not recommended for
documents of high importance.

The second kind is cross cut. Cross cut shredders (DIN P-2 and P-4) have two blades that cut the paper along both diagonals, creating approximately 400 pieces. 
This type of shredder is more private, as there are more pieces, and each piece is smaller. This makes it harder to try and recombine them into the original document.
It is still possible though, so while this can be used for slightly more important documents, it is not recommended for documents of very high importance.

The third type is micro cut. Micro cut shredders (DIN P-5, P-6 and P-7) use their blades to shred pages into very small particles, often so small single letters cannot
be read. Often, P-5 rated shredders create up to 2000 pieces from an A4 page. The size of the pieces that are produced directly lead to the high privacy of the 
shredder because there is almost no way to reassemble them into the original document. These shredders are often used for very high importance documents, including
government documents and financial records.

Source: https://www.recycling.com/types-of-paper-shredders/
